---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Q1 = `nums ← [4, 7, 2]

FOR EACH n IN nums
{
  DISPLAY(n * 2)
}`;
const Q2 = `let items = ["a", "b", "c"];

for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
  console.log(items[i]);
}
console.log(items[items.length]);`;
const Q4 = `aList ← [1, 2, 3]
APPEND(aList, 4)
REMOVE(aList, 2)
INSERT(aList, 1, 0)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 7</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Six questions &middot; 11 minutes &middot; cumulative
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>Five exam-style questions covering Activity 1.1 through 1.25, weighted toward <b>list traversal and safe access</b>. This is the last set before the quarter review.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Set a timer for 11 minutes.</b> Work them out on paper; do not run
            the code. Mark each answer <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>. Guess
            rather than leave a blank &mdash; the AP exam has no penalty for a
            wrong answer.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <p>
            Four have exactly one correct answer, one asks you to select
            <i>all</i> that apply, and the last is about data or the impact of
            computing rather than about code. <b>Thirteen of the exam's seventy
            questions look like that last one</b>, so it is on every set from
            here on.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1</h3>

          <p>What does this display?</p>

          <Code
            code={Q1}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Question 1 — exam notation"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>8 14 4</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>4 7 2</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>13</code></li>
            <li>(D) <code>26</code></li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>What does this code display?</p>

          <Code
            code={Q2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Question 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>a b c</code> then <code>c</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>a b c</code> then <code>undefined</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>a b c</code> then an error stops the program</li>
            <li>(D) <code>a b</code> then <code>c</code></li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3</h3>

          <p>The same off-by-one written in <b>exam notation</b> would do what?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Display nothing extra</li>
            <li>(B) Display <code>undefined</code></li>
            <li>(C) Produce an error message and terminate the program</li>
            <li>(D) Wrap around to the first element</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4</h3>

          <p>After these operations, what is <code>LENGTH(aList)</code>?</p>

          <Code
            code={Q4}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Question 4 — exam notation"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>3</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>4</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>5</code></li>
            <li>(D) <code>2</code></li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>Which are valid ways to visit every element of a list in <b>exam notation</b>?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>FOR EACH item IN aList</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>REPEAT LENGTH(aList) TIMES</code> with a hand-kept index</li>
            <li>(C) <code>for (i = 1; i &le; LENGTH(aList); i++)</code></li>
            <li>(D) <code>REPEAT UNTIL</code> with a hand-kept index</li>
            <li>(E) <code>aList.forEach()</code></li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 6 &mdash; Data and Impact</h3>

          <p>A mapping project asks thousands of volunteers to each trace a few roads from satellite images. This is an example of:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Compression</li>
            <li>(B) Metadata</li>
            <li>(C) The digital divide</li>
            <li>(D) Crowdsourcing</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Six answers, each marked <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, plus how far
            you got when the timer went off. Worth <b>2 points</b>, graded on
            completion.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
